\begin_layout Title
NIST Data Time Tagging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Message
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each timetag bin corresponds to 78.125 ps.
 means that your device uses an ultra-high-speed timing system where each "tick" or unit of recorded time (a bin) represents exactly 78.125 picoseconds (ps).
 [1] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here is how to break down the technical details:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Picosecond (ps):
 One trillionth of a second (10⁻¹² seconds).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Timetag bin:
 A single discrete unit of time used by the hardware to record exactly when an event happened.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* The Math:
 The value of 78.125 ps per bin corresponds to a clock frequency of exactly 12.8 GHz which can be calculated this by taking the reciprocal of the time:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{78.125\times10^{-12}\text{ seconds}}=12.8\text{ GHz}
\]

\end_inset

 [1,
 2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practical terms,
 this gives the time resolution of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are recording events (like photon detections or signal edges) the device measures them in extremely precise 78.125 ps intervals.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This allows for exact chronological reconstruction of fast signals,
 which is highly useful in fields like quantum physics,
 time-of-flight (TOF) experiments,
 and high-speed logic testing.
 [1,
 2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are analyzing a specific dataset (like the NIST Bell Test Data),
 let me know,
 and I can help you convert these raw time-tag integers into standard seconds or nanoseconds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[1] [https://www.nist.gov](https://www.nist.gov/document/bell-test-data-file-folder-descriptions)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[2] [https://uqdevices.com](http://uqdevices.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/Logic16-User-Manual-3.0.pdf)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Message
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If your timetags look completely random or evenly distributed when you subtract them then you are likely looking at uncorrelated event data instead of pulsed synchronization frames.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the NIST loop-hole-free Bell test experiment,
 photons are emitted in discrete periodic intervals defined by a master laser clock but the raw logs require careful processing to see the correlations.
 [1,
 2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Your differences are showing a random distribution because of three common pitfalls in parsing this specific dataset:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
You Are Calculating Absolute vs.
 Relative Differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you subtract the absolute timetag of Alice Event #1 from Alice Event #2,
 the values will look random because the down-conversion process is probabilistic.
 Photons are not generated on every single laser pulse.
 [1] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* The Fix:
 You need to calculate the differences modulo the laser repetition period.
 The NIST experiment used a 100 kHz pulsed laser.
 This means a laser pulse occurs exactly every 10 microseconds (100,000 bins if using a 100 ns window macro-clock,
 or 128,000 bins at 78.125 ps).
 [3,
 4] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Take your absolute timetag,
 calculate timetag % laser_period_in_bins,
 and plot a histogram of those remainders.
 You should immediately see a sharp peak where the true photon arrivals cluster.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Missing the Channel Mapping (Data Demultiplexing)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The raw data files clump multiple channels together into a single bitstream.
 A single 32-bit or 64-bit integer often contains both the timetag value and channel metadata encoded into specific bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you do not mask out the channel metadata bits before doing your math,
 the metadata will corrupt your numbers,
 making your time differences look like completely random,
 massive integers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Fix:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Consult the DataProcessingDescription.pdf file found in the [NIST Bell Test Data Repository](https://www.nist.gov/pml/applied-physics-division/bell-test-research-software-and-data/repository-bell-test-research-0).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
You must use a bitwise AND mask (e.g.,
 raw_value & 0x0FFFFFFF) to separate the pure time data from the channel markers (which identify Alice/Bob choices,
 sync pulses or detector clicks).
 [5,
 6]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Laser Phase Drift and Shared Clock Distances
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alice and Bob are physically separated to close the locality loophole.
 Because their time-tagging hardware modules are independent,
 their internal reference clocks experience linear phase drift over the course of a long data run.
 [1,
 2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* The Fix:
 You cannot directly subtract Alice's absolute timetag from Bob's absolute timetag.
 You must first use the periodic sync pulses (the find_sync.T1 files in the dataset) to build a clock-correction model.
 This aligns Alice's and Bob's timelines using a linear or polynomial fit before calculating cross-coincidences.
 [3] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Are you currently looking at the data from a single party (e.g.,
 just Alice's file) or are you trying to find cross-coincidences between Alice and Bob?
 Let me know,
 and I can give you the exact bitwise operations or laser period values for that file type.
\end_layout

