\begin_layout Section
Message
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Subtracting Alice Event #1 directly from Bob Event #1 will not work for matching sequences in this dataset.
 [1] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary issue is that the arrays do not have a 1-to-1 alignment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because photon pair generation and detection are highly probabilistic Alice might record a photon from a laser pulse where Bob records absolutely nothing (and vice versa).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Subtracting the literal index sequence creates a complete alignment failure by matching unrelated laser cycles.
 [2,
 3] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To properly correlate the two data streams you must shift your methodology from index matching to laser cycle matching (Sync Co-registration):
 [2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Match on Sync Pulses,
 Not Detector Events
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead of working with the detector events files directly,
 locate the companion find_sync.T1 files for that specific run in the [NIST raw data repository](https://www.nist.gov/pml/applied-physics-division/bell-test-research-software-and-data/repository-bell-test-research-3).
 [4,
 5] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* These files track the master laser's continuous 100 kHz clock pulses as recorded independently by Alice's and Bob's time-tagging hardware.
 [6] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* The Math:
 Because the laser pulses are perfectly periodic,
 you can uniquely map Alice's sync pulse array to Bob's sync pulse array.
 Since sync pulses happen on almost every clock cycle,
 you can easily use a sliding cross-correlation or check for stable intervals to align Alice Sync #N with Bob Sync #N.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Correct the Reference Clock Drift
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because Alice and Bob use distinct hardware clocks physically separated across the NIST campus their time counters drift relative to each other over time.
 [7] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Once you pair Alice's sync pulses with Bob's sync pulses,
 calculate their exact time difference.
 You will observe that the difference $T_{Bob
\backslash
_sync} - T_{Alice
\backslash
_sync}$ is not a constant value;
 it drifts linearly or quadratically as the run progresses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Fit a linear regression model to this drift:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta t_{\text{drift}}(t)=m\cdot T_{\text{Alice}}+c
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use this equation to map all of Alice’s timetags into Bob’s time domain before performing any matching:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{\text{Alice\_corrected}}=T_{\text{Alice}}+\Delta t_{\text{drift}}(t)
\]

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Match via a Time Window (Coincidence Window)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once Alice's timeline is corrected and converted to Bob's clock domain you can drop the index-based subtraction completely.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use a time-window lookup approach instead:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Iterate through each corrected event time in Alice's data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Search for any event in Bob's data that falls within a narrow coincidence window (typically 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm10$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm100$
\end_inset

 nanoseconds) of that event.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you find a Bob event within that tiny window you have successfully matched a true entangled photon pair.
 [2,
 3]
\end_layout

