\begin_layout Standard
The NIST setup is highly unique compared to standard lab setups.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a small lab,
 you can run a physical cable from Alice to Bob to share a common clock.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because NIST separated Alice and Bob by hundreds of metres to close the locality loophole a physical cable would introduce too much noise and delay.
 They had to use separate clocks which is why the sync pulses are absolutely vital.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Ada,
 you can handle this efficiently by taking advantage of its strong typing and fixed-point or 64-bit integer types to prevent overflow during time conversions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here is how you can structure your Ada logic to parse the data,
 align the sync pulses,
 and find the true coincidences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Define Your Data Types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 ensure you are using 64-bit integers (Long_Long_Integer) for the timetags to avoid overflow,
 as the raw numbers grow very large over long runs.
\end_layout

