\begin_layout Standard
In the 03_12_CH_pockel_100kHz.run dataset,
 the "sync elements" do not exist as a separate marker file like they do in earlier diagnostic tests.
 Instead,
 they are explicitly embedded directly into the binary hardware streams as specific channel flags.
 [1] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Understanding how these sync elements are structured within the file involves several key details:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Laser "Clock" Channel (Channel 1)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the raw hardware data-tagging modules used by NIST,
 every physical input signal is assigned a channel number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Channel 1 is dedicated exclusively to the master laser sync pulse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The laser fires at exactly 100,000 pulses per second (100 kHz).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Every time the laser emits a pulse an electrical signal is split and sent down separate transit lines to both Alice's time-tagging hardware and Bob's time-tagging hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This physical event registers as an event entry flagged as Channel 1 in both files.
 [1,
 2,
 3]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How the Sync is Encoded in Binary (The Bitmask)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because you are using Ada,
 you need to dissect the exact binary layout of the data stream to pull these sync elements out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each entry in the raw file is packed into a 32-bit unsigned integer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bits are mapped as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 0 to 27 (28 bits):
 The fractional "micro-clock" time-tag value (measured in those exact 78.125 ps bins).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 28 to 31 (4 bits):
 The Hardware Channel ID metadata.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the sync elements in Ada,
 read each 32-bit word and use a bitwise shift right by 28 bits to isolate the channel ID and look for 1:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Type representing a raw 32-bit event word from the .dat stream
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type Event_Word is new Interfaces.Unsigned_32;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Extract the hardware channel (top 4 bits)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Channel_ID :
 constant Interfaces.Unsigned_32 := 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Interfaces.Shift_Right(Interfaces.Unsigned_32(Word),
 28);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Extract the pure 78.125 ps timetag (bottom 28 bits)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Micro_Clock :
 constant Interfaces.Unsigned_32 := 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Interfaces.Unsigned_32(Word) and 16#0FFF_FFFF#;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Channel_ID = 1 then
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- This is a Laser Sync Element!
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Save Micro_Clock into your alignment array.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
elsif Channel_ID = 2 then
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- This is a Single-Photon Detector Click event.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The 100 ns "Macro-Clock" Overflow Words
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because a 28-bit micro-clock incrementing every 78.125 ps overflows and wraps back to zero roughly every 21 milliseconds the NIST hardware injects an external master 100 ns "macro-clock" counter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When reading the data stream you will encounter placeholder entries where bits 28–31 equal 0 or another metadata command flag.
 These are roll-over signals indicating that a 100 ns time block has elapsed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Your parsing loop must continuously maintain a global Current_Macro_Time :
 Long_Long_Integer := 0;
 tracker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Every time you hit a macro-clock increment word,
 add 100_000 (since 100 ns = 100,000 ps) to your macro tracker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When you find a Channel 1 Sync Element,
 its absolute timetag is calculated as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Absolute\_Time}=\text{Current\_Macro\_Time}+(\text{Long\_Long\_Integer}(\text{Micro\_Clock})\times78125/1000)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Summary of what you will see
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By filtering for Channel 1 you will yield a clean sequence of absolute timetags spaced roughly every 128,000 bins (or 10 microseconds) apart.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By matching the first few cleanly recorded Channel 1 syncs in Alice's file to the first few in Bob's file you establish the zero-point anchor and can instantly track how the two clocks drift away from each other over the duration of the 03_12 run.
 [1] 
\end_layout

