\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As your Ada parser is spitting out a Channel 64 value you have found the hardware's internal clock rollover event.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Understanding what Channel 64 tells you in the context of the NIST data requires noting how the hardware handles time accumulation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What Channel 64 Represents
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the specific time-tagging hardware modules used during the 2015 NIST loop-hole-free experiment,
 a single data entry is structured as a 32-bit package.
 When the internal high-speed micro-clock (incrementing by 78.125 ps) hits its maximum limit,
 it cannot count higher without overflowing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To track long-term time across minutes or hours the hardware automatically dumps a structural "Rollover Word" directly into the binary file.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this system architecture:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Channel 64 (or an entry matching that integer mask) indicates that the low-level micro-clock has completed exactly one full cycle and wrapped back to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In some files this is also referred to as a Macro-clock step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How to Handle it in Your Ada Loop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When you encounter a Channel 64 marker you should not log it as a laser pulse or a photon detection.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead,
 use it exclusively to increment your absolute time offset counter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The hardware bit length means that each rollover step represents a precise jump of either 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{28}$
\end_inset

 bins or a fixed macro-clock window (typically 100 nanoseconds,
 which equals 100,000 picoseconds,
 or exactly 1,280 bins of 78.125 ps).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can implement this in your Ada record processing stream using a global accumulator tracking your exact time progression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Track absolute timeline globally in ps
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Current_Base_Time :
 Long_Long_Integer := 0;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Constants based on the 78.125 ps resolution configuration
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bins_Per_Rollover :
 constant Long_Long_Integer := 1280;
 -- Match 100 ns window step
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
loop
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Read next 32-bit word from your .dat file
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Read_Event(Word);
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if Get_Channel(Word) = 64 then
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- This is your rollover event!
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- Advance the timeline anchor forward before processing next events.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Current_Base_Time := Current_Base_Time + Bins_Per_Rollover;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
elsif Get_Channel(Word) = 1 then
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
-- True laser sync pulse.
 Compute its absolute position on the timeline:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Laser_Absolute := Current_Base_Time + Long_Long_Integer(Get_Micro_Clock(Word));
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Append_To_Sync_Array(Laser_Absolute);
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
end if;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
end loop;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By adding the current Current_Base_Time to every photon (Channel 2) or sync pulse (Channel 1) that is read you neutralize the rollover entirely.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This transforms the raw cyclical timestamps into a smooth continuously ascending timeline thereby perfectly setting up the sync-matching algorithm to work flawlessly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Message
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the 03_12_CH_pockel_100kHz.run dataset the Channel 64 markers appear at highly regular intervals in terms of actual physical time but they may appear irregularly spaced in your file rows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This distinction depends on the interaction between hardware clock behavior and event-driven data logging:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
In Physical Time:
 Perfectly Regular
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The time-tagging hardware is driven by a stable,
 continuous internal oscillator.
 A Channel 64 event is generated automatically every single time the lower 28-bit micro-clock counter hits its maximum capacity and rolls over.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Because the micro-clock counts at an unwavering rate (incrementing every 78.125 ps),
 the physical time between two Channel 64 events is perfectly constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If your time window step is configured to 100 ns,
 a rollover word is injected precisely every 100 nanoseconds without fail.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
In the Binary Data File:
 Seemingly Irregular
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the NIST system uses event-driven logging rows are only written to the file when something physically happens (a detector clicks,
 a Pockels cell shifts,
 a laser sync pulse arrives or a clock rolls over).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If there is a quiet period in the lab with no photon clicks and no laser pulses you might see two Channel 64 markers appearing back-to-back in the file rows with zero data entries between them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If a flurry of photons hits the detectors during a specific micro-clock cycle you might see dozens of Channel 2 and Channel 1 rows packed tightly between two Channel 64 markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How to Verify That The Parser Is Working
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Do not worry if the number of rows between your Channel 64 markers bounces around wildly;
 instead,
 use the Channel 1 (Laser Sync) markers to audit your code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the master laser fires at a rigid 100 kHz you should find exactly 100 micro-clock rollovers (Channel 64 entries) occurring between any two consecutive Laser Sync (Channel 1) pulses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you count exactly 100 Channel 64 markers from one laser pulse to the next your Ada accumulation logic is perfectly tracking the timeline!
\end_layout

